@* Barcode's Overview, written by hand after QrCode's: a shipping number (Code 128), products (EAN
   and UPC), size and print, and the name. Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A barcode shows a value as bars for a scanner, with the value printed under them. It's drawn on the server as an SVG, so it needs no script, scales without blurring, and prints. For a link or longer text, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/qr-code">QrCode</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">A shipping or order number</Heading>
<TextBlock>The default type, Code 128, holds any ASCII text: letters, digits, dashes and spaces. It packs runs of digits two to a bar pattern, so number-heavy values stay short. Set <Code>Value</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="barcode-shipping" />

<Heading Level="2">Products</Heading>
<TextBlock>For a retail product, set <Code>Type</Code> to <Code>Ean13</Code>, <Code>UpcA</Code> or <Code>Ean8</Code>. Give the digits without the check digit and it's added, or with it and it's checked: a wrong one is an error, as a mistyped code would scan as another product. The digits print in the standard layout, between the guard bars.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="barcode-product" />

<Heading Level="2">Size and print</Heading>
<TextBlock><Code>ModuleWidth</Code> sets the width of the narrowest bar, 2px by default, and the barcode is as wide as its bars need. In a narrower box it shrinks, keeping its shape, but below about 1.5px per bar a phone's camera may not read it, so give it the room or a shorter value. <Code>BarHeight</Code> sets the bars' height in the same units, 50 by default. The blank margins either side are part of the barcode: scanners need them, so keep other things out of them.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>For print, set <Code>ModuleWidth</Code> in millimetres, <Code>Length.Of("0.33mm")</Code>: that's EAN's standard size, and GS1 permits down to 0.264mm. A full-height EAN-13 at that size has a <Code>BarHeight</Code> of about 69; shorter bars are harder for retail scanners.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>Screen readers hear "Barcode" and the value. To say what it's for, set <Code>Label</Code>, such as "Tracking number": the name becomes "Tracking number, SHIP-2026-0417". When the value is written beside it, set <Code>Decorative</Code>. With no value yet, such as while the page loads it, nothing is drawn; a value the type can't hold, such as an EAN with the wrong check digit, is an error. The name comes from the app's text (<Code>IzzyText.BarcodeLabel</Code>, "Barcode {0}").</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Barcode Value="SHIP-2026-0417" />

        @* A product: the check digit is added. *@
        <Barcode Value="400638133393" Type="BarcodeType.Ean13" />
        """;
}
